It's been five years since Apple last redesigned the &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj;, so it was due for an update. The PROLED &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj; looks a lot like the mini-LED model, but it's thinner and lighter. The 14-inch model is about half a millimeter thinner and weighs just over three pounds, while the 16-inch model is almost a millimeter thinner and weighs four pounds. Apple shaved off about half a pound for the 14-inch model and 0.7 pounds for the 16-inch model, and while that doesn't sound like much, it makes a big difference. You can feel how much lighter it is the second you take it out of the box. Apple brought back the glowing logo for the PROLED &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj;, so everyone around you will know you have the fancy new &zwnj;OLED&zwnj; model.<br/>
<br/>
The &zwnj;OLED&zwnj; display is the major new feature, and it looks as good as you would expect from a display in a laptop starting at &#36;2,899. Blacks are true black, and since each pixel lights up individually, there's none of the bloom you get with mini-LED when viewing bright objects on a dark background. The PROLED &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj; can get super bright too, and that brightness paired with the deep blacks, HDR content looks great. &zwnj;OLED&zwnj; sometimes isn't bright enough outdoors, but that's not the case here.<br/>
<br/>
The display bezels are thinner, and the 16-inch machine is actually 16.3 inches instead of 16.2. There's also an iPhone-like <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/dynamic-island/">Dynamic Island</a> that replaces the notch and houses <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/siri/">Siri</a> AI, Live Activities, and more. The &zwnj;Dynamic Island&zwnj; makes sense because you can now touch the &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj;'s display. It works like touching an iPhone or <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/ipad/">iPad</a>, so you can swipe through webpages, tap buttons, drag and drop, and pinch to zoom.<br/>
<br/>
Touch isn't meant to replace the trackpad and keyboard, but it's there if you want it. Apple made some tweaks to macOS, so menu options expand a little more when you touch them than when you click them, and tiny window resizing buttons get larger. Apple put a sturdier hinge in the PROLED &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj; so the display won't move as much when you tap it, but it's not perfect. It does still jiggle depending on where you tap.<br/>
<br/>
The touch feature is fine, but it's probably not a reason to go out and get the PROLED &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj;. It has the potential to be useful in some situations, but it's not going to dramatically change the way most people use a laptop.<br/>
<br/>
New chips weren't ready in time for the PROLED machines, so they use the same M5 Pro and M5 Max as the mini-LED models. That's a disappointment in a laptop that starts at almost &#36;3,000. Since Apple is still selling the mini-LED models, you're basically paying a &#36;400 premium for the higher-end &zwnj;OLED&zwnj; display, the thinner and lighter design, and the touch functionality.<br/>
<br/>
